      "id": "word-somos--identity",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "somos",
      "answerEn": "we are (an identity or defining description)",
      "noteEn": "Somos is the present form of ser used with nosotros or nosotras, “we.” Ser presents an identity, category, or defining description; estamos normally presents a state or location instead.",
      "example": { "es": "Somos vecinos.", "en": "We are neighbors." },
      "tags": ["word", "ser", "identity", "present", "A1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-pareja--couple",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "pareja",
      "answerEn": "couple; two romantic partners together",
      "noteEn": "Pareja can name two people as a couple or one person's partner. This is the collective sense for the two people together.",
      "example": { "es": "Lucía y Marta son pareja.", "en": "Lucía and Marta are a couple." },
      "tags": ["word", "relationship", "noun", "A1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-me--gustar-experiencer",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "me",
      "answerEn": "marks me as the person who likes something",
      "noteEn": "With gustar, Spanish presents the liked thing as pleasing to a person. Me identifies the speaker as that person; it does not become a separate English word when the natural translation uses “I.”",
      "example": { "es": "Me gusta el café.", "en": "I like coffee." },
      "tags": ["word", "gustar", "experiencer", "pronoun", "A1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-gusta--i-like",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "gusta",
      "answerEn": "I like; is pleasing to me",
      "noteEn": "With me before it, gusta expresses that one thing or situation is pleasing to the speaker. The liked thing controls the verb: use gusta for one thing and gustan for more than one.",
      "example": { "es": "Me gusta este libro.", "en": "I like this book." },
      "tags": ["word", "gustar", "singular", "present", "A1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-ser-role-without-indefinite-article",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "ser + profesión, relación o rol sin un/una",
      "questionEn": "When you say someone's profession, relationship, or role in Spanish, do you always need a word for “a” or “an”?",
      "answerEn": "Often no. Use a form of ser directly before the profession, relationship, or role when it has no added description.",
      "noteEn": "Ser changes to match the person: soy, eres, es, somos, sois, or son. A bare profession or role normally follows without un or una: soy estudiante. An article commonly returns when the noun has an added description or is being singled out: es una estudiante excelente.",
      "example": { "es": "Luis es profesor.", "en": "Luis is a teacher." },
      "tags": ["grammar", "ser", "article omission", "roles", "A1"]
    }
  ],
